{
  "dom_id": "step-q98121",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/687a54314642c2657d0e62d7ad946434dd51ad55e82e1d0eed90d4889ab1bfea.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=3f(-\\frac{1}{2}x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f(-(\\frac{1}{2}x))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=3f(-(\\frac{1}{2}x)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/d5cec0eb8275ee2356786f9836c48f5056264a15ae2880f6342694de78de139e.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-(\\frac{1}{2}x)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/9543db151d716bb2fea3d131e1641cbf753292e62634d11a756cd112e5c28d05.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to  the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=3f(-(\\frac{1}{2}x)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/8081e212181b08ce88e7b71c35b2a2fff52429e7ff4c18351292e20adbe81c9d.png)",
